And I wasn't even invited to the binge!  Hurmpphh!  ;-)

Agreed -- on a bus with a single drive, your points are well founded, and
this is similar to the wretched SCSI vs Ultra IDE debate we both survived on
the AlphaNT list.  It is when you have a machine with many hard drives, that
you will want to make sure that you have an U2W bus with 80MB/sec
_bandwidth_ available.  If you have 4 drives all capable of sending 18MB/sec
realistically, then the effective rate achieved when all drives wish to
communicate on the bus at the same time is reduced on an UW bus to 10MB/sec
per bus due to the 40MB/s bandwidth available.  On a U2W bus, all drives can
deliver their data at their max rates unimpeded by the other devices because
of the 80MB/s bandwidth.  Due to command overhead (negotiation/selection
etc) you'll never achieve the 40MB/s or 80MB/s theoretical maximums, but all
things being equal the U2W bus gives double throughput maximum than a UW
bus.

The IntraServer cards are awesome.  I like the SCSI cards based on the
Symbios chipsets.  It appears that a company called LSI Logic acquired
Symbios (or maybe they just got renamed ?).  Anyway, checkout their 64bit
PCI card with two Ultra3 buses (320MB/s aggregate!):
http://www.lsilogic.com/techlib/io_standard/sym22915.pdf

All their SCSI cards are at:
http://www.lsilogic.com/products/io_standard/ioshabs/iosbddescrp.htm

