"Vanco, Donald" wrote:
>         I'd like to pull the source & compile under 2.2.12, but I'm not sure
> which RPMs comprise the source/headers - can someone send me a pointer
> (ftp://ftp.alphalinux.org/kernel/v2.2/ appears to have kernel images in bz2
> format - but what handles that compression?)

If you're looking to recompile, it's probably best to stay away from
RPM's.  For the kernel source, you can get it in either .gz or .bz2
compressed formats.  .bz2 is smaller (and transfers faster), but you
need a bzip2 uncompressor for it, which are still relatively uncommon.

Pretty much every Linux machine has gunzip, so go ahead and get the
.gz'd version.  Same thing.  :-)
 
>         Also - what's the appropriate way to shut down this system?  It
> never seems to respond to init 0 or 6 with what I expect to see on an Intel
> box.

I'm a fan of "halt" or "reboot"... the "shutdown" command will also help
(see the man page).
 
>         What Internet browser is available for the Alpha?  I tried to load
> Mozilla but ran into all kinds of issues with missing RPMs that I could not
> locate for installation.  Is there a (stable) Netscape version for Alpha?

Be a man; use lynx ;-)

Seriously, if you have access to various DEC Unix libs, you can get a
"real" Netscape working.  That's what I ended up doing here.
 
>         I'm an X newbie - what should I read?  I see a lot of perms errors &
> cores.....

If you're using GNOME and friends, make sure you're running the latest
version... for Red Hat Linux 6.0, errata are at
http://support.redhat.com/.  The difference was astounding for me -- my
work PC has been going since September 10th without any GUI crashes, vs.
about 4 hours with the stock GNOME.  -rt


